# Dashboard window

The window loads an absolute path to `web/index.html` with `js_api=DashboardApi`. A relative `url` makes pywebview start its own server.

A new page action is a public method on `DashboardApi` plus a call through `api()` in `web/app.js`. Method names stay public; pywebview skips `_` names. The page polls `get_snapshot`.

The page is `web/index.html`, `web/app.css`, `web/app.js`, and `web/history.js` (the History screen and the weather location dialog). Keep it plain: no npm, framework, bundler, chart library, or extra listener. Charts are inline SVG drawn in `history.js`, with the categorical `--series-*` colors in fixed order by miner.

The History screen reads `get_history`. The display loop hands each round of readings to `history.HistoryRecorder`, which writes `history.db` next to `config.json` every 10 minutes. Tests that touch it run under a temporary config.

```python
# BAD
webview.create_window("Groundhog Gamma Tuner", url="web/index.html")

# GOOD
page = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "web", "index.html")
webview.create_window(
    "Groundhog Gamma Tuner",
    url=page,
    js_api=DashboardApi(self),
)
```

```javascript
const bridge = api();
if (bridge) bridge.start_autotuner();
```
